I promised not to get into this, but I'm low on self control.

XML _looks_ bulky, but if you transmit and store it as a compressed
file, all those plethora of tags from a small set of choices you'd use
compress really, really well with a gzip or bzip2 or maybe 7zip file
format, so you end up pretty much a wash by _compressed_ volume
compared to other ASCII file formats.

The bigger advantage of XML over Joe's Screendoor and ASCII Format
Company file format is that XML is now pretty much a done deal, the
whole industry is converging like ships around the Maelstrom, sucked
in by the virtues of having _one_ common format, perfect or not, most
suscinct or not. And there's nothing so huge about bones files that you
can't put one on a floppy disk in compressed XML anyway.

